            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

For an older house with old original windows, it is actually reasonably air tight. There are a few key areas that will become more "air tight" as you complete your planned renovations:



** Air leakage associated with the older windows &amp; doors



** Around the attic access panel (can be sealed with a removable caulking, such as Draft Attack)



** Up through the crawlspace below (it is heated space but still seems to have leakage to the outside)



** An intentional hole is the 6" chimney for the current furnace (that could be eliminated with a furnace upgrade)



** There is another intentional hole - the 5" pipe above the furnace into the attic - I am unsure if this was designed to be a combustion air supply or outside fresh air supply for the furnace. If you upgrade the furnace to a high efficiency, direct vent unit, this 5" pipe can then be directly connected to the return air ductwork of the new furnace (with an integrated manual damper) to provide fresh air to all room of the house each time the furnace operates.



Savings: As this house is reasonably air tight now &amp; will require some additional strategic ventilation after the renovations, air leakage reductions will only provide a moderate amount of savings. Please read the Ventilation Sections of this report.



There is, however some rebates you can receive for air leakage reductions if you are participating in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). For homeowners participating in the DHERP, a minimum 10% reduction in air leakage would qualify for a $ 500 rebate. Achieving 20% qualifies for $750, and a minimum 30% reduction qualifies for $1,000.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior main floor walls of your house are 2x4 (R12) and I have modeled the addition of another R20 (eg. 4" of extruded polystyrene rigid foam board) insulation to the exterior of the main floor walls.



Under the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019, there are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+).



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R20, you could potentially save $ 516 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 21.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 8,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP).



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <foundation>

Your crawlspace is heated space and the walls are bare concrete. I had emailed you an Infrared Image of the outside of these walls glowing orange (with heat loss) while it -21C outside !!! I have modeled the addition of a minimum R24 insulation (rigid foam board, spray foam, etc) to the interior of these concrete crawlspace walls. You can still have the crawlspace seasonally vented in the spring &amp; summer, but make sure those 2 vents are sealed &amp; covered with insulation during the heating season.



Savings: By adding R24 of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation (or spray foam) to the interior of your crawlspace walls, you could potentially save $ 1,072 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 44.4 GJ, and qualify for up to a $ 2,000 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade.



Note: Adding R10 - R23 to 100% of the crawlspace walls would reduce the rebate to $1,000 ........ and the energy savings would decrease as well. 



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior above foundation wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



</foundation>
                <windows>

All of the windows in this house (8) are older windows that could be upgraded in conjunction with your planned exterior wall insulation &amp; siding project. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of these older windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings:

By replacing these 8 older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 232 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 9.6 GJ, and qualify for up to $ $2,000 in rebates (8 existing windows @ $250 each) through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior above foundation wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</windows>
                <doors>

Both of the exterior doors in this house are older wooden doors that do not close well (and the rear door has a single pane of glass for a window). I recommend you replace these doors with new Energy Star steel insulated units with a minimum R value of 5.5, and at least double pane windows (triple is better) with low E coatings and argon gas fill between the panes.



Savings: By replacing these 2 older doors with higher efficiency Energy Star rated units (as outlined above), you could potentially save $ 83 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 3.4 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new doors from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</doors>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 50 USG electric hot water tank. As there has been an increase in houses &amp; businesses in Fort Smith using propane as a primary fuel source (regular delivery from Hay River), we discussed an option for you to consider replacing this hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a Uniform Energy Factor of 0.91 or higher.



Although I quite like using electricity for heating &amp; hot water in "hydro electric" communities of the NWT (as there are no associated GHG's), I am often asked to illustrate the estimated cost of alternative fuels - as the homeowner looks for cost savings. Most Fort Smith residents are already using an alternative fuel for their primary heating appliance (oil or propane furnace or boiler), so performing this optional calculation for your hot water use is often requested. 



Savings: By replacing your current electric hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing propane-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of a minimum 0.91 UEF, could potentially save $ 751 per year in energy costs and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with a very old oil furnace with a default efficiency of 82.7%.  As there has been an increase in houses &amp; businesses in Fort Smith using propane as a primary fuel source (regular delivery from Hay River), we discussed an option you may want to consider when you are ready for a future furnace replacement. Have a look at an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Savings: By replacing your current furnace with a 95% AFUE high-efficiency condensing gas furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 322 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 32.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). If you not to complete a DHERP eligible wall insulation upgrade, you would still be eligible for up to $ 1,500 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



Yes ....... I realize the estimated fuel savings of only $322 sounds low. This is because fuel prices tend to have alot of fluctuation and fuel oil prices were quite low (compared to propane) when we completed our most recent Fuel Cost Library in December or 2020 (fuel rates for Fort Smith are summarized later in this report). If I used our previous 2019 fuel cost rates, the annual savings above would have been $ 1,391. The Arctic Energy Alliance is not promoting fuels based on their costs - we try to consider a balance between fuel costs, efficiency of the heating equipment available and the overall GHG reductions that can be achieved.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms for DHERP, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has a toilet that is an older unit that uses a lot of water &amp; it should be replaced with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush !!



Savings:

By replacing an older toilet with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



The upgrade recommendations outlined within this report are estimated to qualify for up to $19,500 in rebates. The following are the estimated rebates per component of the DHERP:



     - $8,000 for the wall insulation

     - $2,000 for the crawlspace wall insulation

     - $2,000 for replacing 8 windows with new eligible units

     - $3,000 for installing an eligible furnace upgrade

     - $   500 for a 10% air leakage reduction

     - $4,000 for the bonus rebate (a potential 55% reduction in total energy use)



NOTE: If you do not proceed with an eligible main floor wall insulation upgrade, than the DHERP does not apply - and the only rebates from the list above that would still apply would be the crawlspace wall insulation and the furnace, however these would only be 50% of what is listed above.



In addition to the estimated DHERP rebates outlined above, you are also eligible for other rebates from Energy Efficiency Rebate Program (http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program), including the following items that may be of interest to you for this house:

     - On-Demand Hot Water (minimum 0.91 UEF) - $750

     - ECM domestic water circulation pump - 50% up to $175

     - Programmable Thermostat - 25% up to $175

     - LED Lighting - see section further down.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



VENTILATION



Your home currently has the following exhaust ventilation:

        - A kitchen range hood fan - does not vent to the outside

        - 1 bathroom fan

        - A clothes dryer (not connected when I was there)



I would recommend you replace the bathroom fan with a new ENERGY STAR?????? rated unit. You may want to consider installing a timer switch (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so it will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat. As your house could use a little more ventilation, you could even install an automatic timer switch to turn this bathroom fan on/off a few times per day for 20-30 minutes.



As outlined in the previous Air Sealing section of this report, your house is reasonably air tight and is expected to become tighter if you proceed with the upgrade recommendations in this report. If your house was being built today (including the renovation upgrades outlined in this report), it would have a "full house" Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) installed.



Almost all houses from the 60's &amp; 70's would not have had an HRV and instead would manage fresh air supply &amp; indoor air quality by using exhaust fans (kitchen, bathroom, clothes dryer) and incorporate a 5" fresh air supply pipe connected to the return air plenum of the furnace - that would allow outside air each time the furnace operates or when an exhaust fan is operating.



As you continue with your "Renovate Tight" upgrades, you will also want to ensure you "Ventilate Right". I think you can achieve adequate ventilation if you replace the main floor bathroom fan with a new ENERGY STAR?????? rated unit (outlined above).



If you ever notice excess condensation building up on the main floor windows, you may need to have a 5" fresh air duct brought into the return air plenum of the furnace (the hole in the ceiling above the current furnace ?). This way, each time the furnace (or the bathroom fan or clothes dryer) operates, a little bit of outside fresh air is drawn into &amp; distributed throughout the house using the furnace supply ductwork. That fresh air supply pipe should have an internal damper that you can manually adjust between fully open &amp; fully closed. This way you can open it a bit further as condensation forms on windows (or the indoor air quality seems stale with odours) or close it further if there are no window condensation issues and the inside of the house feels dry with alot of static electricity.



Savings: I mentioned in the air sealing section of this report there would be about $25 of annual savings by eliminating the uncontrolled air leakage locations. Adding some additional "controlled" ventilation (outlined above), is estimated to cost about $25 annually. You may look at this and say "Why bother, just leave everything as it is". The problem is that the ventilation created by the air leakage into walls, ceilings &amp; attics creates moisture related damage to your house over many years ........... sometimes creating significant mould &amp; rotting in the walls &amp; ceiling of the house. It is always better to eliminate the "bad" leakage and replace it with "controlled" ventilation (ie: an Energy Star HRV or other exhaust fans + a dedicated fresh air supply into the furnace plenum). Your occupancy will be more enjoyable &amp; you will have more control over the amount of ventilation the house (and the occupants) receives.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Fort Smith, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



ECM PUMPS



Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house (up to 2 per fiscal year) you can qualify for $ 175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (justthe bathroom fan, as the kitchen range hood only recirculates air inside the house, and the clothes dryer was not connected), indicated a pressure drop of only 0.3 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not currently expected to draw combustion products into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current furnace and wood stove are both chimney vented and could be susceptible to back drafting if the negative pressure created in the house was greater than 5 Pa.



Your current furnace draws its' combustion air from inside the house. The furnace upgrade option outlined previously would no longer require a chimney &amp; would draw its' combustion air from outside of the house. The Arctic Energy Alliance always recommends that Wood &amp; Pellet Stoves have outside dedicated combustion air supply pipes installed that are directly connected to the back of the stove - and we would recommend this for your wood stove.



During my assessment of your home, I noticed smoke detectors but did not see any carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Fort Smith



The following Dec. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Smith:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.26 per kWh

Oil  - $0.90 per litre 

Propane - $0.73

Wood - $262.50 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $7.46 per bag, $299.62 per tonne bulk delivered



